hy – **Justification Gap**.\n\n   - **Conclusion of Upper‑Bound:** From the above, \\(k=2\\) cannot be realized, while \\(k=0,1,3\\) remain possible. The logical flow is correct, but the missing detailed arguments constitute **Justification Gaps** rather than a logical flaw.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\)**  \n\n   - **Claim:** “Vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points.”  \n     **Verification:** For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), the column \\(x=a\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\). The vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains all of them. The \\(n\\) vertical lines are distinct and none is sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis). Hence the construction is valid. No gap.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\)**  \n\n   - **Claim:** “Verticals \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) plus a sunny line through \\((n,1)\\).”  \n     **Verification:** The vertical lines cover all points except \\((n,1)\\). We must choose a line through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\). For example, the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{1-n}= -\\frac1{n-1}\\), which is admissible for \\(n\\ge3\\). This line is distinct from the verticals and is sunny. Thus the construction works. The solution does not exhibit the explicit slope, so the existence of such a line is left implicit – **Justification Gap**.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=3\\)**  \n\n   - **Claim:** “Three sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points, verticals cover the rest.”  \n     **Verification:** The six points in the last three columns are  \n     \\[\n     (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\ (n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\ (n,1).\n     \\]\n     One possible choice of three sunny lines is:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: \\text{through } (n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2) \\quad (\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n     L_2 &: \\text{through } (n-2,2)\\text{ and }(n,1) \\quad (\\text{slope }-1/2),\\\\\n     L_3 &: \\text